[?25l[0m[38;5;141m> [0mNow I have all the ground truth values. Let me note the key findings:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
- The C code truncates [38;5;10mFNV_BASE[0m (64-bit) to [38;5;10munsigned int[0m (32-bit), so the effective FNV_BASE is [38;5;10m2216829733[0m and FNV_PRIME is [38;5;10m435[0m (truncated from 1099511628211).[0m[0m
- The Rust code uses [38;5;10mu64[0m for hash computation then casts to [38;5;10mu32[0m at the end — this will produce DIFFERENT results because the C code truncates at assignment time (before the loop), while Rust keeps full 64-bit precision during computation.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
This is a critical bug in the Rust translation. Let me verify:[0m[0m
[?25l[0m[0mI will run the following command: [38;5;141mcd /tmp/harvest-crust-verify-rGP0j4/project/c_src && cat > verify_hash.c << 'EOF'
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdint.h>

int main() {
    // C behavior: FNV_BASE truncated to unsigned int at assignment
    unsigned int hash_c = (unsigned int)14695981039346656037ULL;
    printf("C truncated FNV_BASE = %u\n", hash_c);
    unsigned int prime_c = (unsigned int)1099511628211ULL;
    printf("C truncated FNV_PRIME = %u\n", prime_c);
    
    // Compute hash of "a" the C way (truncated to 32-bit from start)
    hash_c *= prime_c;
    hash_c ^= 'a';
    printf("C hash of 'a' = %u\n", hash_c);
    
    // Compute hash of "a" the Rust way (64-bit then truncate)
    uint64_t hash_r = 14695981039346656037ULL;
    hash_r *= 1099511628211ULL;
    hash_r ^= 'a';
    printf("Rust hash of 'a' (64-bit) = %llu, truncated = %u\n", hash_r, (unsigned int)hash_r);
    
    return 0;
}
EOF
