Wait no — [38;5;10mkv.key[0m is of type [38;5;10m&'a mut T[0m, and [38;5;10mkey[0m is of type [38;5;10m&mut T[0m. [38;5;10mstd::mem::swap(kv.key, key)[0m — the first argument is [38;5;10m&mut &'a mut T[0m (since kv.key is a field), and the second is [38;5;10m&mut &mut T[0m. Actually, [38;5;10mstd::mem::swap[0m takes [38;5;10m&mut T, &mut T[0m. So [38;5;10mstd::mem::swap(kv.key, key)[0m where both are [38;5;10m&mut T[0m — this swaps the [38;5;10mT[0m values, not the references.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
So the swap logic is:[0m[0m
1. [38;5;10mswap(*conflict_kv.key, *kv.key)[0m → ck2 gets old_key_val, kv.key's target (k1) gets 0[0m[0m
2. [38;5;10mswap(*conflict_kv.value, *kv.value)[0m → cv2 gets old_val, kv.value's target (v1) gets 0[0m[0m
3. [38;5;10mswap(*kv.key, *key)[0m → k1 gets value from k1b (1), k1b gets value from k1 (0)[0m[0m
4. [38;5;10mswap(*kv.value, *value)[0m → v1 gets value from v1b (59), v1b gets value from v1 (0)[0m[0m
[0m[0m
So after all swaps: k1=1, v1=59, k1b=0, v1b=0. The HAMT node's key reference still points to k1 (value 1) and value reference still points to v1 (value 59). That matches the output![0m[0m
[0m[0m
So the issue is that the HAMT always keeps references to the FIRST inserted key/value locations. When there's a conflict, the new values are swapped INTO the old locations. The new key/value locations (k1b, v1b) end up with the old conflict values (0, 0 in this case).[0m[0m
[0m[0m
This means in the big test, after a conflict at i=7, [38;5;10mkeys[7][0m has value 0 (not 1). So when we search with [38;5;10mkeys[7][0m, we're searching for 0, which was never inserted. That's why the search fails![0m[0m
